.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
.DS_Store

# Local deployment config. `config.example.toml` / `identity.example.toml` are
# the committed templates; the LIVE files are all three untracked now.
#
# `config.toml` was tracked until 2026-09-07 (operator decision) on the
# argument that it holds no secret values — `api_key_env` names the variable,
# never the key. What made that untenable was not secrecy, it was collision:
# the live file is a bind-mount source that the operator edits in place, so a
# tracked copy meant every merge touching it fought an uncommitted working
# change, and each session had to stash-merge-restore-diff by hand to avoid
# losing an edit. Untracked, `config.toml` belongs to the deployment and
# `config.example.toml` carries the shape.
#
# The cost, stated so nobody rediscovers it: master no longer records what is
# actually deployed, and `config.example.toml` is the only thing keeping a new
# key reviewable. `tests/test_env_template.py` is what holds the two in step —
# keep it that way, because `extra = "forbid"` turns a drifted example into a
# container that will not boot.
#
# Legacy pre-TOML names are kept ignored too, so a leftover config.yaml or
# persons.yaml (which held real phone numbers) on an operator's machine can
# never be committed by accident.
/config.toml
# GENERATED PER EVAL RUN, never pre-generated and never committed: the
# deployment's own behaviour values applied over `config.example.toml`, so an
# eval subject composes the Nora that is deployed rather than a fresh install
# (`design/evaluation/01-boundaries.md` §5). It carries the deployment's real
# values -- the doctrine canary, room ids, internal endpoints -- which is
# exactly what `config.toml` is untracked for.
/config.evaluation.toml
/config.yaml
/identity.toml
/persons.yaml

# Phase rollback backups (backups of gitignored/live state files)
*.pre-toml
data/*.pre-alembic
.serena/

# Node (deploy/whatsapp-bridge): dependencies are rebuilt by npm ci / the
# Dockerfile; the tree is never a build input worth committing.
node_modules/

# signal-cli release tarball — fetched+verified by deploy/signal-cli/fetch.sh
deploy/signal-cli/*.tar.gz
# .asc is COMMITTED deliberately: the Dockerfile verifies the tarball against
# it at build time, so it is part of the build inputs, not a fetched artifact.
policy.toml

# Agent worktrees — transient, and embedded git repos besides.
.claude/worktrees/
.worktrees/

.eval-cache/

# The build-time version stamp (DEC-79). GENERATED, never committed: it is
# derived from git history by scripts/gen_version_manifest.py at build time, so
# a committed copy would be a second, stale answer to "what am I running" —
# exactly the disagreement the build gate exists to rule out. Its absence in a
# source checkout is why `version` answers `unavailable` outside a built image.
nora/version.json
RELEVANCE_GATE_TASK.md
.venv

# Voice-wake runtime artifacts — REGENERATED, never committed (the same rule
# as nora/version.json: a committed copy is a second, stale answer to "what
# model is running"). The ONNX is a trained artifact from
# ~/workspace/voice-lab (v3 export, ~205 KB); the seam fixture is PCM audio
# frames for the codec test. Both are produced/buildable, so leaving them
# untracked costs nothing and keeps the identifier gate's "no binaries in
# this repo" invariant intact.
deploy/voice-wake/tests/fixtures/seam_frames.bin


# The embedder service's model files (docker-compose.yml `embedder`,
# EMBEDDER_MODELS_DIR): gigabytes of GGUF, never committed.
/models/

# OpenWiki public reader build output
docs-site/node_modules/
docs-site/dist/
docs-site/.astro/

/secret.toml
.secret-delivery/
/secret-migration-*/
